<!-- ================================================================ -->
<!-- 1. INTRODUCTION ≈ 1200-1500 words.                               -->
<!-- ================================================================ -->

<section xml:id="sec-introduction">
  <title>Introduction</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Conventional autograded questions usually retain an endpoint: the option
      selected, the number entered, or the final correctness verdict.  A fully
      logged card-to-zone drag-and-drop item retains substantially more of the
      act of responding.  A learner may place individual cards, revise earlier
      placements, return cards to the tray, check the current arrangement,
      respond to feedback, reset the board, leave the page, and resume later.
      These records create an outcome layer about submitted answers and a
      process layer about the paths that produced them.
    </p>

    <p>
      The process layer is not immediately observable as a sequence of complete
      responses.  In the Runestone export studied here, a move row records one
      card and its destination, whereas a check row records the complete
      submitted configuration.  The logs identify cards and zones using
      positional labels rather than authored content, and the answer key is
      defined in the PreTeXt source rather than repeated in every event row.
      Consequently, a valid trajectory must be constructed by aligning the
      source and log, ordering and decoding the events, and replaying the
      incremental changes.
    </p>

    <p>
      This preprocessing is part of measurement rather than a neutral clerical
      step.  A missing move, ambiguous ordering, stale item source, malformed
      checked state, or inappropriate attempt boundary can alter the derived
      trajectory and every feature computed from it.  Analyses that treat the
      raw rows as an already valid action sequence risk attributing processing
      artifacts to learners.
    </p>
  </introduction>

